ALVA — Add Watch Mode (CPU vs CPU Spectator Mode)

Implement a complete spectator mode named “Watch Mode” in the Alva repository.

Before changing code, inspect the existing architecture and preserve its patterns, visual language, accessibility, keyboard/gamepad navigation, battle rules, and tests. Do not create a second combat implementation. Watch Mode must use Alva’s existing Battle, Fighter, CombatAIController, roster, stage, HUD, navigation, and difficulty systems.

Current architecture to preserve

The repository currently has:

* Home actions in js/screens/home-screen.js.
* Quick Battle flow:
    Home → Select Mode → Select Difficulty → Select Fighter → Select Stage → Battle.
* Shared selection state in js/core/app.js.
* Difficulty definitions and AI profiles in js/data/difficulty.js.
* Shared fighter roster in js/ui/fighter-roster.js.
* Setup headers in js/ui/components.js.
* Stage selection in js/screens/map-select-screen.js.
* Battle DOM/UI orchestration in js/screens/battle-screen.js.
* Match simulation in js/game/battle.js.
* CPU combat intelligence in js/game/combat-ai.js.
* Screen/back-stack navigation in js/core/screen-manager.js.
* Screen sections in index.html.

Currently Battle creates:

* P1 with PlayerController.
* P2 with CombatAIController.

The existing tests already demonstrate that P1 can be replaced with another CombatAIController and the complete battle simulation continues to work. Build Watch Mode around that capability instead of duplicating battle logic.

⸻

Feature

Add a new Home action:

Watch Mode

This is Alva’s CPU-vs-CPU spectator mode.

Place the Watch Mode button in the Home action stack between Play and Discover, while preserving Practice Ground. It should use the same Home action styling and navigation behavior as the existing secondary actions and have the normal right-chevron treatment.

The intended Home hierarchy is:

Play → Watch Mode → Practice Ground → Discover

If responsive CSS changes the visual layout, preserve this DOM/navigation order.

Watch Mode should be a direct Home mode. Do not send the user through the existing Select Mode screen.

Clicking Watch Mode begins this flow:

Watch Mode → Difficulty → CPU 1 Fighter → CPU 2 Fighter → Stage → Watch Battle

There are exactly four setup steps:

1. Difficulty
2. CPU 1
3. CPU 2
4. Stage

The selected difficulty applies equally to both CPUs.

Do not add separate CPU 1 and CPU 2 difficulty settings.

⸻

Watch Mode selection state

Do not overload Quick Battle’s existing characterId in a way that causes the two modes to overwrite each other.

Give Watch Mode its own selection state, preferably grouped separately, for example conceptually:

selection.watch = {
  difficulty: DEFAULT_DIFFICULTY,
  cpu1CharacterId: defaultAvailableCharacterId,
  cpu2CharacterId: defaultAvailableCharacterId,
  mapId: MAPS[0].id,
}

The exact structure can follow the project’s conventions, but these requirements are mandatory:

* Watch selections must not corrupt Quick Battle selections.
* Quick Battle must still remember/use its existing difficulty, characterId, and mapId.
* Going Back during Watch setup must preserve already chosen Watch values.
* CPU 1 and CPU 2 may choose the same fighter. Do not artificially prohibit mirror matches.
* Invalid/stale fighter, stage, or difficulty values should continue to use the project’s normal fallback behavior.

